<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Defs>

  <!--
    Eras are SimWorld's translation of RimWorld's TechLevel ladder into "civilization through the ages": a
    civilization-wide age that completes once every research project tagged to it (ResearchProjectDef.era)
    is finished. order is the ladder position (0 = earliest); techLevel is the TechLevel band the era
    occupies for CostFactor and, later, faction/settlement generation. Exotic's techLevel is Archotech, the
    top of RimWorld's ladder, standing in for both "Ultra" and "Archotech" until SimWorld's own translation
    of that end of the ladder is designed.

    An era does not multiply threat points. A threatPointsFactor ladder (1.0 to 3.4) did until 2026-11; it
    bought raiders of unchanged strength and nothing else, so it was removed. See
    StorytellerUtility.DefaultThreatPointsNow for the evidence, and docs/research/era-arc.md finding 5 for
    how era pressure is meant to return (as strength, not head count).
  -->

  <!--
    Era pacing (docs/status.json research translation; docs/research/era-arc.md section 9).

    Intent: each era lasts long enough for the settlement to build in it and live in it, and the whole ladder
    spans a long game. Translated: the years a settlement spends with each era current are
      SticksAndStones 2, Agrarian 3, Bronze 4, Classical 5, Medieval 6, Industrial 7, Information 8,
    so Exotic turns at about year 35 and is lived in from there, with the endless tail behind it.
    A year is 60 days.

    citizensPerScholar sets the research benches a settlement wants in its current era (one per that many
    citizens, rounded, at least one). Not sourced: a more advanced settlement spares a larger share of its
    people for study. A nominal settlement of 25 gets 1, 1, 1, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 benches across the eras.

    researchCostFactor multiplies every project in the era, on top of RimWorld's tech-level factor. Derived,
    not tuned per seed: the time a settlement spends with era X current is the time it takes to research the
    era after it (cheapest-first within the frontier window sweeps nearly all of it), so
    SticksAndStones itself is the band's starting kit, not something it waits for: fire, shelter and cooking
    gate the first house and stove, and priced up they left a founding band without a roof for two months
    (era-pacing 60-day bench: 20/2024 lost all 21 people to a day-18 heat wave with no house built). It keeps
    factor 1, is researched in its first days as before, and the two years go to Agrarian:
      factor(X+1) = years(X) x 60 x points per day at scholars(X) / (authored cost of X+1 x tech factor)
    with the tech factor RimWorld's 1.5x per tech level above the researcher's. Points per day are measured,
    not modelled. Research is the lowest-priority work (WorkTypeDef naturalPriority 300, as in RimWorld), so a
    bench is staffed by whoever has nothing more pressing:
      one bench:   about 600 points a day once its researcher has learned (era-pacing probe, band 20 seed 99,
                   with era-survival's 4,000-tick sittings merged: 358, 520, then 660-680 a day by month)
      two benches: about 390 a bench (docs/research/era-arc.md runs A-D: some 147,000 effective points to the
                   Medieval turn by day 150-213)
      three or more: assumed 390 a bench as well. Not measured: no played run has had three benches.
      SticksAndStones               4,500 x 1.0, the starting kit       -> 1.0
      Agrarian                     12,100 x 1.0, 2 y,   600/day -> 6.0
      Bronze                       21,110 x 1.5, 3 y,   600/day -> 3.4
      Classical                    38,060 x 1.0, 4 y,   600/day -> 3.8
      Medieval                     60,930 x 1.0, 5 y,   600/day -> 3.0
      Industrial                  120,210 x 1.5, 6 y,   780/day -> 1.55
      Information                 161,370 x 1.5, 7 y, 1,170/day -> 2.0
      Exotic                      163,680 x 2.0, 8 y, 1,560/day -> 2.3
    Generated endless projects carry the last era's factor (Research.EraPacing.CostFactorFor). EraPacingTests
    recomputes the nominal years from the loaded content and pins them to the intent, not these literals.
  -->

  <EraDef>
    <defName>SticksAndStones</defName>
    <label>sticks and stones</label>
    <description>Fire, stone tools, and the first words spoken around a hearth.</description>
    <order>0</order>
    <techLevel>Neolithic</techLevel>
    <researchCostFactor>1.0</researchCostFactor>
    <citizensPerScholar>25</citizensPerScholar>
  </EraDef>

  <EraDef>
    <defName>Agrarian</defName>
    <label>agrarian</label>
    <description>Farming, storage, and permanent settlement replace the hunt and the chase.</description>
    <order>1</order>
    <techLevel>Neolithic</techLevel>
    <researchCostFactor>6.0</researchCostFactor>
    <citizensPerScholar>25</citizensPerScholar>
  </EraDef>

  <EraDef>
    <defName>Bronze</defName>
    <label>bronze age</label>
    <description>Smelting and alloying open the door to tools and weapons stone cannot match.</description>
    <order>2</order>
    <techLevel>Medieval</techLevel>
    <researchCostFactor>3.4</researchCostFactor>
    <citizensPerScholar>20</citizensPerScholar>
  </EraDef>

  <EraDef>
    <defName>Classical</defName>
    <label>classical</label>
    <description>Law, mathematics, and monumental building organize a civilization at scale.</description>
    <order>3</order>
    <techLevel>Medieval</techLevel>
    <researchCostFactor>3.8</researchCostFactor>
    <citizensPerScholar>18</citizensPerScholar>
  </EraDef>

  <EraDef>
    <defName>Medieval</defName>
    <label>medieval</label>
    <description>Iron and steel, castles and cathedrals, and the long road toward gunpowder.</description>
    <order>4</order>
    <techLevel>Medieval</techLevel>
    <researchCostFactor>3.0</researchCostFactor>
    <citizensPerScholar>12</citizensPerScholar>
  </EraDef>

  <EraDef>
    <defName>Industrial</defName>
    <label>industrial</label>
    <description>Steam, steel mills, and the factory line remake how a civilization builds.</description>
    <order>5</order>
    <techLevel>Industrial</techLevel>
    <researchCostFactor>1.55</researchCostFactor>
    <citizensPerScholar>8</citizensPerScholar>
  </EraDef>

  <EraDef>
    <defName>Information</defName>
    <label>information</label>
    <description>Electronics, computing, and orbit turn a civilization's knowledge outward.</description>
    <order>6</order>
    <techLevel>Spacer</techLevel>
    <researchCostFactor>2.0</researchCostFactor>
    <citizensPerScholar>6</citizensPerScholar>
  </EraDef>

  <EraDef>
    <defName>Exotic</defName>
    <label>exotic</label>
    <description>Beyond the authored tree: the far end of the ladder, where SimWorld's later procedural extension picks up.</description>
    <order>7</order>
    <techLevel>Archotech</techLevel>
    <researchCostFactor>2.3</researchCostFactor>
    <citizensPerScholar>5</citizensPerScholar>
  </EraDef>

</Defs>
